# The build context is copied into the image, and anything in a layer is recoverable from
# that image by anyone who pulls it. `docker history` and `docker save` do not care that a
# later layer deleted a file. So this is the same problem as .gitignore and needs answering
# separately, because Docker does not read .gitignore.
#
# This file denies everything and then allows back what the image needs, rather than
# listing what to keep out. Two reasons, and the second is the one that matters.
#
# It fails safe. A deny-list has to be updated every time private material is added, and
# the first build of this image shipped three planning documents because it had not been.
# An allow-list excludes anything new by default; the failure mode is a missing file and a
# build that breaks loudly, rather than a shipped file and a build that looks fine.
#
# It names nothing private. This file is committed and publishes with the repository, so a
# deny-list here announces the directories it is trying to hide -- which is the same reason
# the private names live in .git/info/exclude rather than in .gitignore. There is nothing
# to learn from the list below except what the image runs.

*

# The examples, one directory per architecture or production concern.
!00-config-runtime/
!01-reflex-agents/
!02-goal-based/
!03-utility-based/
!04-learning/
!05-multi-agent/
!06-parallelization/
!07-nlp-foundations/
!08-production-patterns/
!09-model-portability/
!10-drift/

# The shim, the recordings, and the tier-to-model table.
!shared/

# The source pages the code is drawn from, and the notebooks that link back to them.
!reference/
!colab/

# Read at runtime by nothing, but a container someone is exploring should carry its own
# documentation.
!README.md

# Required rather than optional. MIT says the notice ships with any copy or substantial
# portion of the software, and an image is a copy: someone who pulls it and never sees the
# repository has still received the code.
!LICENSE

# Referenced by docker-compose for the per-agent image variant.
!Dockerfile.agent

# Re-excluded inside the allowed trees: caches are rebuilt in the image, and bytecode
# compiled against a different interpreter than this image has is worse than none.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
# Stray run output should not enter the image. The one exception is the critic run log,
# which two published claims cite as their raw evidence -- it is tracked in git for the
# same reason, so both surfaces carry it and neither carries anything else.
**/*.log
!10-drift/critic-experiment/run-2026-08-03-sonnet5.log
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ipynb_checkpoints/
